  </metadata>

  <prompt>🔴 THIS TASK'S ORIGINAL PREMISE IS RETIRED — DO NOT BUILD A FOURTH ROOT SET. The owner ruled on 2026-09-09 (task 028) that a service request IS core but is NEVER externally grantable. `CallerPrincipal` composes exactly THREE externally-grantable root sets, and that is correct, not a gap. Adding `AccessibleServiceRequestIds` was considered and REFUSED: it would be composed from `sprk_externalrecordaccess` rows that cannot exist, producing an always-empty set that encodes the false claim into the principal surface.

WHAT REMAINS IN SCOPE is the genuine over-denial filed as ISS-003 (#964): `GetTodoRootAsync` has no service-request arm, so a to-do parented to a service request resolves to `None` and is denied for EVERYONE — including the workforce user who submitted that very service request and can see it in their own widget. 🔴 The SHAPE of the fix is an OPEN OWNER DECISION (what may a requester DO to their own service request's to-dos — read? write? create?). Do not improvise it. Re-scope this POML to the owner's answer before writing code.</prompt>
  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. BFF authorization internals: CallerPrincipalResolver strategies, AccessibleRecordSetService, evaluator. Follow ADRs strictly.</role>
  <goal>The ISS-003 over-denial is closed by the mechanism the owner selects, WITHOUT making service requests externally grantable: `CallerPrincipal` still composes exactly THREE externally-grantable root sets, `GrantSupportedRootEntities` still contains exactly { sprk_project, sprk_matter, sprk_workassignment }, and no `AccessibleServiceRequestIds` set exists anywhere. Service-request scoping continues to run through the EXISTING `service-requests` ExternalModuleDescriptor (`ExternalAccessModule.cs`), which scopes by `sprk_requestedby` and is fail-closed on the plane — a CIAM partner gets an empty set server-side. Tests pin both the fix and the three negatives.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🔴 **"CORE" AND "EXTERNALLY GRANTABLE" ARE TWO DIFFERENT LISTS.** The spec's Owner Clarifications say "Core: project, matter, work assignment, service request", and that sentence is what produced this task's retired premise: it reads as though all four are grantable to externals. They are not. A service request is core in the sense that nothing else confers access to it (it is not a child that inherits via an ancestor) — and is NEVER grantable to an external contact. The doc was the thing that was wrong; the code was already right in both halves. See notes/task-028-service-request-root.md §3 and the project CLAUDE.md.

**The decisive evidence, and it cost one metadata query**: `sprk_externalrecordaccess` carries lookups for `sprk_project`, `sprk_matter`, `sprk_workassignment`, `sprk_invoice`, `sprk_organization` and `sprk_contact` — and NO service request. A child can *point at* a service request (seven ways), but no external grant can ever *name* one. When a task says "add the fourth X", check first whether the data model has a place to put it.

**Ground truth at HEAD** (unchanged by this correction): `GrantSupportedRootEntities` = { sprk_project, sprk_matter, sprk_workassignment } (AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:99-104); both strategies fill exactly 3 root sets (CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:91,97,102). **This is the correct shape — it is not a gap to close.**

**Service-request scoping ALREADY EXISTS by the correct, different mechanism**: the `service-requests` ExternalModuleDescriptor in `ExternalAccessModule.cs`, shipped 2026-08-10 by the sibling project `spaarke-SPA-external-access-platform-r2`. It scopes by **requester** (`sprk_requestedby`), which is the right model for an internally-submitted record, and it is fail-closed on the plane: a CIAM partner gets an empty set server-side, so "internal-only" does not depend on the client hiding a tab. 06 §F1's rule ("a ScopeDimension can only point at a set that exists on the principal") is satisfied by the module, which is the same mechanism.

**What is genuinely broken — ISS-003 (#964)**: `/api/v1/external/todos/{id}` serves BOTH planes, and `GetTodoRootAsync` has no service-request arm, so a to-do parented to a service request resolves to `None` and is denied for everyone, including its own submitter. It needs a DIFFERENT rights shape from the other three roots: they answer "is this id in my set?" synchronously from `CallerPrincipal`; a service request answers "is `sprk_requestedby` me?", which is a Dataverse read the to-do path does not currently make. Bolting that into `RightsForRoot` would put an I/O call behind a signature that is pure everywhere else — which is why the shape is an owner/design decision, not a patch. Currently unexercised: zero service requests exist in dev.

NOTE: Phase 1/2 will have reshaped these files (evaluator returns recordId-&gt;rights; FR-20 impersonated source) — read the AS-MERGED shapes before editing.</background>
